🤖 [Qwen Edge] To sort an array in Java, you can use several methods depending on whether the sorting needs to be done in place (no new array allocation) or not.

### 1. Using `Arrays.sort()`
This method sorts the elements of an array and modifies it directly.
```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class SortArrayExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Integer[] numbers = {2, 4, 7, 3, 9};

        System.out.println("Before sorting: ");
        printArray(numbers);

        Arrays.sort(numbers);

        System.out.println("After sorting: ");
        printArray(numbers);
    }

    public static void printArray(Integer[] array) {
        for (int i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
            System.out.print(array[i] + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

### 2. Using `Collections.sort()`
This method sorts the elements of an array and returns a new sorted array.
```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class SortArrayExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Integer[] numbers = {7, 3, 5, 1, 9};

        System.out.println("Before sorting: ");
        printArray(numbers);

        Integer[] sortedNumbers = Arrays.copyOf(numbers, numbers.length);
        Arrays.sort(sortedNumbers);

        System.out.println("After sorting (returned array): ");
        printArray(sortedNumbers);
    }

    public static void printArray(Integer[] array) {
        for (int i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
            System.out.print(array[i] + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

### 3. Using `Arrays.sort()` with a custom comparator
If you need to sort an array based on a specific criteria, you can create a custom comparator.
```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class SortArrayExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] strings = {"apple", "banana", "cherry"};

        System.out.println("Before sorting: ");
        printArray(strings);

        Arrays.sort(strings, new Comparator<String>() {
            @Override
            public int compare(String s1, String s2) {
                return s1.compareTo(s2);
            }
        });

        System.out.println("After sorting (based on alphabetical order): ");
        printArray(strings);
    }

    public static void printArray(String[] array) {
        for (int i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {
            System.out.print(array[i] + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

### Summary
- `Arrays.sort()` sorts the elements in place and modifies them.
- `Collections.sort()` returns a new sorted array, which is useful if you want to preserve the original array or if sorting has side effects (like modifying an ArrayList).

Each method comes with its own trade-offs depending on whether you need to modify the original data structure and how much memory you're willing to allocate.

